575
00:31:26,221 --> 00:31:31,460
It kills by airborne radiation and contamination.

576
00:31:31,560 --> 00:31:33,660
Scientists here agree that the best defense

577
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:36,220
against a radioactive weapon is to find it early

578
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:39,160
and identify it using this.

579
00:31:39,260 --> 00:31:41,960
The RadScout's a portable radiation detection tool.

580
00:31:42,060 --> 00:31:45,280
It will detect gamma rays and neutron radiation,

581
00:31:45,380 --> 00:31:48,480
do the analysis on board, and tell the person operating

582
00:31:48,580 --> 00:31:51,020
it what the material, what likely material produced

583
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:55,420
that radiation to allow us to begin doing a threat analysis.

584
00:31:55,421 --> 00:32:00,860
Traces of uranium, plutonium, the likely tools of a terrorist,

585
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:04,320
can quickly be isolated from normal background radiation

586
00:32:04,420 --> 00:32:06,960
with this handheld device.

587
00:32:07,060 --> 00:32:09,760
The Rad Scout's forte is its portability.

588
00:32:09,860 --> 00:32:11,760
So once a threat has been called in

589
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:13,580
or there's a suspicion of a threat,

590
00:32:13,680 --> 00:32:14,980
the Rad Scout is an excellent tool

591
00:32:15,080 --> 00:32:17,980
to take down the halls, up and down the high rise,

592
00:32:18,080 --> 00:32:20,120
looking for elevated radiation.

593
00:32:20,220 --> 00:32:22,080
That's using it in its detector mode.

594
00:32:22,180 --> 00:32:24,480
Once we've found a spot with elevated radiation,

595
00:32:24,481 --> 00:32:26,240
we can then do the analysis.

596
00:32:26,340 --> 00:32:28,180
And so it's very good at searching areas

597
00:32:28,280 --> 00:32:30,080
and identifying the threat.

598
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:32,320
In this laboratory trial, the threat

599
00:32:32,420 --> 00:32:35,720
could be hidden anywhere or nowhere.

600
00:32:35,820 --> 00:32:37,320
The first thing I have to do with the Rad Scout

601
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:39,420
is get a measure of the background of radiation.

602
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:41,140
Everywhere in the world, there's radiation,

603
00:32:41,240 --> 00:32:43,340
but it varies from location to location.

604
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:47,040
As I walk through here, I'm just watching a bar chart here

605
00:32:47,140 --> 00:32:49,540
that's telling me how much radiation is present.

606
00:32:49,640 --> 00:32:52,280
But I'm just looking for that bar chart to go up, which

607
00:32:52,281 --> 00:32:55,820
which tells me there's something radioactive here.

608
00:32:55,821 --> 00:32:57,980
Once the investigator calibrates the detector

609
00:32:57,981 --> 00:33:01,160
for this location, he can then hunt for unusual levels

610
00:33:01,161 --> 00:33:03,800
of radiation, indications that something dangerous

611
00:33:03,801 --> 00:33:05,720
might be hiding here.

612
00:33:05,721 --> 00:33:08,520
There's about 50 counts per second of gamma radiation

613
00:33:08,521 --> 00:33:09,520
coming here.

614
00:33:09,521 --> 00:33:11,740
So now what I'll do is I'll go looking for regions

615
00:33:11,741 --> 00:33:15,240
where there's more radiation than the background.

616
00:33:15,241 --> 00:33:18,900
And as I walk through here, I'm seeing small variations

617
00:33:18,901 --> 00:33:20,200
in the radiation.

618
00:33:20,201 --> 00:33:23,940
As I get in here, I see increased radiation.

619
00:33:24,040 --> 00:33:26,840
And over here, I see significantly increased radiation.

620
00:33:26,940 --> 00:33:28,740
And so now I've got a reason to think there's something

621
00:33:28,840 --> 00:33:31,340
in this file cabinet that's at a higher level,

622
00:33:31,440 --> 00:33:33,240
that's producing a higher level of radiation.

623
00:33:33,340 --> 00:33:35,440
And I'd like to go understand what that is.

624
00:33:35,540 --> 00:33:39,160
The RadScout's touch screen activates the search mode.

625
00:33:39,260 --> 00:33:42,120
And it's already starting to tell me sources of radiation

626
00:33:42,220 --> 00:33:43,160
that it could be.

627
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:46,560
And now it's identified thorium as a source of radiation

628
00:33:46,660 --> 00:33:48,000
that it has identified.

629
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:49,560
And again, with my training, I understand

630
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:52,060
is a common component in some optical lenses.

631
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:53,460
And so there's a chance that there's

632
00:33:53,560 --> 00:33:55,160
just an optical component in here.

633
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:57,060
And so we'll open up the file cabinet

634
00:33:57,160 --> 00:33:59,900
and look for the actual piece of material.

635
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:02,500
And here you can see an optical component that gets used

636
00:34:02,600 --> 00:34:04,900
routinely around the laboratory that has radioactive thorium

637
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,120
in it at a very small level.

638
00:34:07,220 --> 00:34:10,040
The trace levels of radioactive thorium detected here

639
00:34:10,140 --> 00:34:12,520
do not pose any real health hazard.

640
00:34:12,620 --> 00:34:15,560
But if a well-concealed dirty bomb had been planted here

641
00:34:15,660 --> 00:34:18,760
instead, the Rad Scout would have likely detected it

642
00:34:18,761 --> 00:34:23,000
and given security workers time to evacuate.

643
00:34:23,100 --> 00:34:26,340
But when terror strikes, there isn't always a clear way out,

644
00:34:26,440 --> 00:34:28,640
especially from a burning high rise.

645
00:34:28,740 --> 00:34:30,980
To answer this need, there is a new technology

646
00:34:31,080 --> 00:34:34,940
that promises to help victims safely escape by jumping off.

647
00:34:39,920 --> 00:34:41,920
The explosion went off around 9 a.m.

648
00:34:42,020 --> 00:34:44,520
And we could feel the explosion.

649
00:34:44,620 --> 00:34:47,320
April 19, 1995.

650
00:34:47,321 --> 00:34:51,260
At 9.02 a.m., a rental truck packed with homemade explosives

651
00:34:51,261 --> 00:34:52,760
